      detail: "Typical range, AUD ex GST. Several systems, write tools with confirmation, security review and multi-client testing. Roughly 25 to 55 engineer-days.",
    },
    {
      name: 'Customer-facing product server',
      range: 'From $80k',
      detail: "Typical range, AUD ex GST. Multi-tenant authorisation, per-tenant limits, versioned public tool surface and documentation for your customers. Scoped after paid discovery.",
    },
  ],
  comparison: {
    rightFor: [
      "Several AI clients, such as Claude, Copilot, IDE assistants and your own agents, need access to the same systems",
      "You want staff to use approved AI assistants against live business data with their own permissions",
      "You sell software and want customers to connect it to the AI assistant of their choice",
      "You want to avoid rebuilding integrations every time you change model or AI vendor",
      "Your security team needs one governed, logged interface for AI access instead of many ad hoc ones",
    ],
    notRightFor: [
      "Only one application will ever call the system, where a direct API integration is simpler",
      "The system has no API and the data is not ready; data engineering or an API layer comes first",
      "A vendor already ships a maintained MCP server for the product and it covers your needs",
      "You need autonomous multi-step work rather than access; that is agent development, which may use MCP underneath",
      "Your policy does not yet allow AI assistants near the data in question; governance should come first",
    ],
  },
  definitions: [
    {
      term: 'Model Context Protocol (MCP)',
      definition: "An open standard, built on JSON-RPC 2.0, that defines how AI applications connect to external systems to discover and call tools, read resources and use prompt templates.",
    },
    {
      term: 'MCP Server',
      definition: "A service that exposes capabilities to AI applications over MCP. It advertises tools, resources and prompts, and executes requests from connected clients.",
    },
    {
      term: 'MCP Host and Client',
      definition: "The host is the AI application a person uses, such as a desktop assistant or IDE. Inside it, an MCP client manages the connection to each MCP server.",
    },
    {
      term: 'Tools, Resources and Prompts',
      definition: "The three kinds of server feature in MCP. Tools are functions the model can execute, resources supply context and data, and prompts are templated messages and workflows for users.",
    },
    {
      term: 'Streamable HTTP',
      definition: "The standard MCP transport for remote servers, where each message is an HTTP POST to a single endpoint and replies return as JSON or a request-scoped event stream. The alternative, stdio, is for locally launched servers.",
    },
    {
      term: 'Protected Resource Metadata',
      definition: "A document, defined in RFC 9728, that a remote MCP server must publish so clients can discover which authorisation server issues its access tokens.",
    },
  ],
  relatedServices: [
    { title: 'AI Agent Development', href: '/services/ai-agent-development' },
    { title: 'API Development', href: '/services/api-development' },
    { title: 'LLM Integration', href: '/services/llm-integration' },
    { title: 'AI Chatbot', href: '/services/ai-chatbot' },
    { title: 'Cybersecurity', href: '/services/cybersecurity' },
